    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nHi @echasnovski,\r\n\r\nThank you so much for writing this, your work is amazing, your code is beautiful and very well thought out and documented, I've yet to explore anything other than `mini.surround` but the amount of functionality in your framework is mind blowing and the mini name does it injustice, surround by itself could be it's own plugin/ecosystem with recipes and configurations, etc.\r\n\r\nI certainly didn't expect something with `mini` in it's name to have so much functionality, supporting anything I could imagine with custom lua handlers/regexes, coming from vim-surround I had no idea how useful the highlighting feature  and  `cover_or_next` could be.\r\n\r\nThe plugin as is is practically perfect for my needs so feel free to close this issue, this is just something that came up in my testing while migrating from `vim-surround` and is definitly in the \"nice to have\" category.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>I use surround-like configuration/keybinds:</summary>\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nsurround.setup({\r\n  -- vim-surround style mappings\r\n  -- left brackets add space around the text object\r\n  -- 'ysiw('    foo -> ( foo )\r\n  -- 'ysiw)'    foo ->  (foo)\r\n  custom_surroundings = {\r\n    ['('] = { output = { left = '( ', right = ' )' } },\r\n    ['['] = { output = { left = '[ ', right = ' ]' } },\r\n    ['{'] = { output = { left = '{ ', right = ' }' } },\r\n    ['<'] = { output = { left = '< ', right = ' >' } },\r\n    S = {\r\n      -- lua bracketed string mapping\r\n      -- 'ysiwS'  foo -> [[foo]]\r\n      input = { find = '%[%[.-%]%]', extract = '^(..).*(..)$' },\r\n      output = { left = '[[', right = ']]' },\r\n    },\r\n  },\r\n  mappings = {\r\n    add = 'ys',\r\n    delete = 'ds',\r\n    find = '',\r\n    find_left = '',\r\n    highlight = 'gs',     -- hijack 'gs' (sleep) for highlight\r\n    replace = 'cs',\r\n    update_n_lines = '',  -- bind for updating 'config.n_lines'\r\n  },\r\n  -- Number of lines within which surrounding is searched\r\n  n_lines = 30,\r\n\r\n  -- Duration (in ms) of highlight when calling `MiniSurround.highlight()`\r\n  highlight_duration = 2000,\r\n\r\n  -- How to search for surrounding (first inside current line, then inside\r\n  -- neighborhood). One of 'cover', 'cover_or_next', 'cover_or_prev',\r\n  -- 'cover_or_nearest'. For more details, see `:h MiniSurround.config`.\r\n  search_method = 'cover_or_next',\r\n})\r\n\r\n-- Remap adding surrounding to Visual mode selection\r\nvim.api.nvim_set_keymap('x', 'S', [[:<C-u>lua MiniSurround.add('visual')<CR>]], { noremap = true })\r\n\r\n-- Make special mapping for \"add surrounding for line\"\r\nvim.api.nvim_set_keymap('n', 'yss', 'ys_', { noremap = false })\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n\r\nAs you can see from my config above I'm using the left brackets as a special customization for spaced surroundings (as per your recommendation in the docs) and it works great.\r\n\r\nThat said, IMO, the below use cases can be improved:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- 'ysiw('\r\ntest -> ( test )\r\n-- 'cs()' could be improved to remove the spaces, since\r\n-- ) is the no-spacing bind the result should be '(test)'\r\ntest -> ( test )\r\n-- 'cs((' results in extra spaces (repeatable)\r\ntest -> (  test  )\r\ntest -> (   test   )\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnother thing I noticed is the support for tags with properties:\r\n```lua\r\n-- ysiwtp class=\"important\", results in\r\n<p class=\"important\">test</p class=\"important\">\r\n-- whereas with vim-surround the result would be\r\n<p class=\"important\">test</p>\r\n```\r\n> another side effect of the above invalid tag is that you can no longer use `dst` and `cst` as it fails with an error:\r\n```text\r\n(mini.surround) No surrounding 't' found within 30 lines and `config.search_method = 'cover_or_next'`.\r\n```\r\n\r\n",
